## 5. Redundancy

Kill-signature groups (identical set of mutants killed):
  {B01, B11, R01} — all three kill nothing; {B08, R07} — both kill {M02, M03, M11}.

- B01: no unique discrimination (B02–B12 all cover the bare-line token-adjacent path)
  and no unique rule element. Redundant as a discriminator; it is the canonical
  positive baseline (8/15) and removing it loses no mutant kill.
- B11: no mutant or probe reacts to its only distinctive text
  (K_HELD_BACKED_STRICT): P_no_strict_split survives 0/21, i.e. truncation changes no
  outcome. Redundant for discrimination; it is the only case carrying the STRICT token,
  so it is retained as the syntactic witness of step 3, but it forces nothing.
- B08 and R07 have identical kill signatures. B08 is strictly dominated by B07; R07 is
  dominated by B07/R02/R03/R05/R09. They differ only in text shape (bare backticked
  line vs table row); one of the two is redundant as a discriminator.
- Not redundant despite zero kills: R01 is the only case that reaches step 4b (no token
  in cell[2] -> last group in region). B09 and R06 are indispensable (sole killers of
  M08 and M09). R02 is the sole discriminator for first-vs-last-after-token and for
  non-digit skipping (both probes killed by R02 alone).

## 6. Disagreements / defects (frozen files — reported, not edited)

a) Battery docstring provenance is false for R01 and R02. It says they are "the verbatim
   SCORECARD lines 693 and 667 of the freeze base"; both are truncated copies:
   - R02 omits `; `C15_TOOL_ROWS` 0/18 → 18/18; C15 `uncovered` → `uncovered`` from cell[2],
     shortens the pointer cell, shortens both hashes to `dac9590`/`99911e8`, and truncates
     the verdict cell.
   - R01 omits `; the round-73 C10 claim is now falsified-closed, not carried` from the
     verdict cell.
   Effect on labels/kills: none measured. Scoring the full rows in-process:
     R01: full verbatim row label=0 reference=0 kills=-
     R02: full verbatim row label=0 reference=0 kills=M02,M03,M04,M05,M06,M07,M10,M11
   The labels stay 0 and the mutant kill sets are unchanged, so this is a provenance
   defect in a frozen comment, not a mislabel.

b) `reports/round-91/mutants.reference` is not a file in the lane tree. The rule in code
   is the `reference()` function inside reports/round-91/mutants.py; that function was
   scored (0/21). Reported so the artefact name in the task is not mistaken for a file.

c) M02 and M03 are indistinguishable on this battery: both kill exactly
   {B04,B05,B07,B08,R02,R03,R05,R07,R09}. Both die, so the predicate holds, but the
   battery does not separate "left side of arrow" from "first raw N/D in region".

d) Rule elements with no discriminating case (measured in §2): step-1 last-line
   selection; step-2 >=5-cell threshold and trailing-`|` condition; step-3 STRICT
   truncation (inert); the score-before-pair reading of step 4a; step-4b `last` and its
   right-side preference; step-6 coverage-map/held anchoring. Eight probe mutants survive
   0/21 on these elements; none of the 11 frozen mutants depends on them, so C1's frozen
   predicate is unaffected, but a v3 implementation could break any of them and still
   score 0/21.

e) Labels the rule does not force: none. 21/21 forced by the stated substrings above.

## 7. Verdict for this seat

Battery labels: 21/21 re-derived as forced, 0 unforced. reference: 0/21. Mutants: 11/11
killed; declared kill cases 11/11 correct. Defects: §6a (provenance wording), §6b
(missing file name), §6c (M02/M03 share a signature), §6d (eight undiscriminated rule
elements). None of these makes a battery label wrong; §6a and §6d are the two that
should be cited upstream.
